WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
The shotlist is not about the 
physical reality initially,

00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:06.256
and not about the
physical reality

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:08.550
of where you're going to
shoot about what the sets are,

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.051
about what the
location is and stuff.

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:12.846
The shotlist is your
reminder of what's

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.472
important in the story.

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:26.943
Hey, everybody.

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:30.113
Oh my god, and what are
Dan Sasaki and George

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:31.489
Joblove doing in here?

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:33.283
I mean, it's like--

00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:35.118
I get uncomfortable
speaking to people

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.786
who are smarter than I am.

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:39.039
But in any event, here we go.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.249
Thank you so much,
and I'm really

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:43.960
happy to be here and doing
this for the Academy,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.005
and happy to meet all of you.

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:49.299
What I do want to
talk about, though,

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:51.843
is where does style come from.

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:55.972
Because it really
comes from inside you.

00:00:55.972 --> 00:01:00.143
You have within you
what you need in order

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:01.644
to create your own
personal style,

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:03.146
and we're going to
talk a little bit

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:06.232
and show you demonstrations
about how you can bring that

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:09.360
out of yourself so
that you can become

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.114
a creative person that actually
functions in this industry.

00:01:13.114 --> 00:01:16.367
But let me tell you
this first of all--

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:20.371
I will bring up,
periodically, 11 observations.

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:23.875
I don't really have rules, but
I do have observations of things

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:27.921
that I feel are important
to keep in mind as you

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.631
go through this industry.

00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:32.217
Observation number
one, the main thing

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:37.263
is to keep the main
thing the main thing.

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:39.432
What do I mean by that?

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:40.475
What I mean is this.

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:44.104
When you get into the
chaos of production,

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:46.940
it's very easy to
forget the point

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.942
of why you're doing something.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.903
It's very easy to
just get blinded

00:01:51.903 --> 00:01:54.114
by all the questions
and all the chaos

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:57.033
and everything that's
going on around you.

00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.911
And then you forget to
get a critical shot that

00:01:59.911 --> 00:02:03.373
tells a piece of the story, or
you forget to get a reaction,

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:07.252
or you forget that,
oh, I didn't set up

00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:09.546
that light for the
main actress who

00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:12.298
likes to be lit from the
side or whatever it is.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:15.969
So when I get a script, I
immediately start shotlisting.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:19.681
And what I mean "immediately,"
I don't give it the once over

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:21.683
and read the whole
thing, just scan it,

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.476
and then go back to
the beginning and start

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.018
shotlisting.

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:29.691
I start shotlisting as soon as
I open it, page one, scene one.

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:32.610
I read the scene, and then
I start shotlisting it

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.655
because in the process of
starting to shotlist it,

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:39.826
I'm reminding myself what the
story is and I'm telling myself

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.661
this is important.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:47.000
When the man walks into the
room and he sees, in the corner,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.460
something tucked
away, OK, well I

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.130
know I need to be able to
see him come in the room,

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:52.964
searching.

00:02:52.964 --> 00:02:56.426
I need a shot of
whatever the object is.

00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:58.887
It becomes the story.

00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:01.890
And I've had so many
directors-- because I've

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.893
shot as a cinematographer
for hundreds of directors--

00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:07.437
and I've listened to every
single excuse about why

00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:09.731
they can't make a shotlist.

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:13.693
And honestly, I don't buy it
because the shotlist is not

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.946
about the physical
reality initially,

00:03:16.946 --> 00:03:18.615
and not about the
physical reality

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:20.909
of where you're going to
shoot about what the sets are,

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:22.619
about what the
location is and stuff.

00:03:22.619 --> 00:03:25.413
The shotlist is your
reminder of what's

00:03:25.413 --> 00:03:27.832
important in the
story, the things

00:03:27.832 --> 00:03:33.129
you need to remember when you're
on set that you need to tell.

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:34.422
That's what the shotlist is.

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:37.175
So I start shotlisting
right away.

00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:41.721
And people are always amazed
when I do direct television

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.224
episodes, that on my
first day of prep,

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:46.142
when I walk into the
studio in the morning,

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:49.437
I have a complete shotlist
of the entire episode

00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:51.522
if I got the script
the night before.

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:54.234
It only takes four to six
hours to do a shotlist

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:58.112
for a one-hour
episode of television.

00:03:58.112 --> 00:04:01.324
And so how much
information do you

00:04:01.324 --> 00:04:04.452
need in your initial shotlist?

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:06.246
Let me show you one of mine.

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.916
This is from an episode that
I directed of Pretty Little

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:10.416
Liars.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:11.501
And I will tell you this--

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.753
I know a lot of you are
from different disciplines.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.713
There are some
cinematographers, some people

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:18.716
want to be directors,
some production designers,

00:04:18.716 --> 00:04:19.717
a whole bunch of things.

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:22.262
What I'm going to tell
you kind of encompasses

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:24.806
what everybody does
in this industry,

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:30.979
so I think it will
hopefully all be valuable.

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:35.400
So this is a script,
a page of a script

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.610
from an episode I directed
of Pretty Little Liars.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.406
And let's concentrate on Scenes
31 and 32, Spencer's barns.

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:44.784
Fairly simple scene, which is
why I'm using it as an example.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:48.162
"Spencer looks around the
ominously empty living room."

00:04:48.162 --> 00:04:52.166
OK, so when I read a script, I
immediately key on to things,

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:53.334
descriptions like that.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:54.836
"Ominously empty."

00:04:54.836 --> 00:04:56.129
OK, that means something.

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:59.632
That suggests maybe a
certain visual treatment.

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:01.926
"She hurries to the bedroom.

00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:04.178
She sees all of
Caleb's stuff is gone.

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:06.681
His side of the closet is empty.

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:07.974
His drawers too.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.726
Her eye's drawn to the bed.

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:13.229
Propped against the pillows is
a framed picture of Goya's Dog.

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:14.814
Caleb left it there for her.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.524
Spencer tenderly
picks up the picture,

00:05:16.524 --> 00:05:18.318
sits on the edge of
the bed, staring at it.

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:22.613
She feels everything and
nothing at the same time."

00:05:22.613 --> 00:05:23.990
OK.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:29.370
So when I read that, this
very simple two scenes

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.663
suggested things to me.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:35.585
It suggested to me that
there's an emptiness in her.

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:37.378
There's a framed
picture of Goya's Dog.

00:05:37.378 --> 00:05:38.129
What does it mean?

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:39.339
I don't know.

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:40.548
I have no idea.

00:05:40.548 --> 00:05:43.968
But I know that it's important
because they've singled it out.

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:46.179
So somewhere, in the
course of this show,

00:05:46.179 --> 00:05:48.639
that image became important.

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:51.184
So I need to make
sure that I see it.

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:54.395
And what I'm looking for
are those kinds of details,

00:05:54.395 --> 00:05:58.274
those kinds of insights into
the psychological makeup

00:05:58.274 --> 00:06:01.402
of the character and how
the character's feeling,

00:06:01.402 --> 00:06:06.616
and then how do I translate that
into a shot that will tell that

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:08.785
a part of the story.

00:06:08.785 --> 00:06:12.872
So here we see Scenes 31 and 32.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.916
I wrote in off angle,
full shot of Spencer

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:17.418
entering the house, a
wide shot of Spencer

00:06:17.418 --> 00:06:20.296
with the empty house
in the foreground

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:22.215
so that she'd be small.

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:24.842
I had the blackness of
the closet revealing

00:06:24.842 --> 00:06:27.553
Spencer, the door opening,
the close of the postcard

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:31.682
on the bed, and then a high
angle, a wide shot of Spencer

00:06:31.682 --> 00:06:33.768
on the bed.

00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:36.062
Those were the
setups that I felt

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:41.192
were going to convey the emotion
that character was feeling

00:06:41.192 --> 00:06:45.238
in the script at
that moment in time.

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:50.451
And let me show you the
scene as it was in the show.

00:06:54.831 --> 00:06:56.124
Caleb?

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:00.795
Caleb?

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:28.698
Now right about now,
you should be thinking,

00:07:28.698 --> 00:07:30.867
oh my god, that's
alarmingly simple.

00:07:30.867 --> 00:07:32.368
Yes.

00:07:32.368 --> 00:07:34.912
Yes, it's alarmingly simple.

00:07:34.912 --> 00:07:38.374
It's a reflection of your
impulse of how you felt

00:07:38.374 --> 00:07:39.959
the story needed to be told.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:44.130
And that is the essence
of where style comes from.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.551
That is the essence of how you
develop your personal style is

00:07:48.551 --> 00:07:52.263
how you interpret the
character seeing the world

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:55.475
or feeling the world
as they go through it.

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:57.602
And what happens then?

00:07:57.602 --> 00:08:01.147
You make your initial shotlist.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:06.360
I give my short list
immediately to the first AD

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.446
because it helps them schedule.

00:08:08.446 --> 00:08:12.241
They're like, oh my god, so
in this quarter page scene,

00:08:12.241 --> 00:08:15.286
you're thinking you may
have as many as 12 setups.

00:08:15.286 --> 00:08:16.871
So they allow enough
time when they're

00:08:16.871 --> 00:08:19.415
making their initial schedule.

00:08:19.415 --> 00:08:23.127
The cinematographer
looks at it and says,

00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:26.005
OK, if you want to do a shot
that goes under the table,

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:27.590
we may need a rig
and stuff like that.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:30.134
And they can start putting it
on their special equipment list

00:08:30.134 --> 00:08:31.219
if they don't carry it.

00:08:31.219 --> 00:08:34.555
The production designer
says, oh, OK, you're

00:08:34.555 --> 00:08:37.767
going to shoot from
inside the closet.

00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:40.186
Means we may have to take
off the back of the closet.

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:42.522
I have to check the set
and see if that's possible,

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:43.356
and stuff like that.

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:45.691
So the wheels start
turning initially.

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:50.196
Now, once you do your scouts
and everything like that,

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:53.074
you're going to adapt and
change your shotlist according

00:08:53.074 --> 00:08:56.035
to the realities, the physical
realities, the logistics

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:57.245
of what you're dealing with.

00:08:57.245 --> 00:09:00.039
And that's where,
if you saw the--

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:01.541
here, I'll bring it up again.

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:06.337
On my shotlist, I have
indecipherable hieroglyphics

00:09:06.337 --> 00:09:08.172
on one side.

00:09:08.172 --> 00:09:12.218
There are little drawings that
only I really can understand,

00:09:12.218 --> 00:09:15.888
but they become helpful
reminders for me.

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:20.351
If you see all those
scratches on the right,

00:09:20.351 --> 00:09:25.273
sometimes it's overhead
diagrams with camera placements

00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:28.442
in relation to actors,
and sometimes it's

00:09:28.442 --> 00:09:31.362
actual frames to
remind myself of kind

00:09:31.362 --> 00:09:34.865
of, compositionally,
what I was thinking of.

00:09:34.865 --> 00:09:37.326
But they're little
pencil sketches.

00:09:37.326 --> 00:09:39.787
I would never venture
to call them storyboards

00:09:39.787 --> 00:09:40.621
because they're not.

00:09:40.621 --> 00:09:42.790
But they're little
reminders for me

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:47.837
based on the actual
physicality of what I've seen.

00:09:47.837 --> 00:09:48.546
Look at this.

00:09:48.546 --> 00:09:52.216
This is a piece of a script
from a movie that I shot

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:55.553
called The Town That
Dreaded Sundown.

00:09:55.553 --> 00:10:00.641
If you look at the bottom
right, where it says "On screen,

00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:03.185
a scene from the
grainy, jumpy movie.

00:10:03.185 --> 00:10:04.604
Audience screams, cheers.

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:06.397
Are watching The Town
That Dreaded Sundown.

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:10.234
There's a drunk old
man, Charles Pierce Jr."

00:10:10.234 --> 00:10:13.487
And then the next page, we
have two teenage boys, Roy

00:10:13.487 --> 00:10:15.906
and Johnny, walk to
the concession stand.

00:10:15.906 --> 00:10:18.200
We settle on Corey and Jami.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:19.327
They have a conversation.

00:10:19.327 --> 00:10:21.662
It's their first date.

00:10:21.662 --> 00:10:25.333
There is a reverend
hawking things,

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:28.961
and a bunch of church ladies.

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:30.838
And reverend is
saying real people

00:10:30.838 --> 00:10:35.384
died as Corey and Jami leave.

00:10:35.384 --> 00:10:37.053
OK, so that's the scene.

00:10:37.053 --> 00:10:42.725
So when Alfonso and I were
talking about this movie

00:10:42.725 --> 00:10:46.020
and we were walking through
all the beats, the story

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:50.191
beats, that had to be conveyed
at the beginning of this movie,

00:10:50.191 --> 00:10:52.610
it was essentially an
introduction to characters.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:55.071
So we need to introduce
people, but we

00:10:55.071 --> 00:10:57.531
have no idea what the impact
of these people are yet.

00:10:57.531 --> 00:11:01.535
We have no idea
who affects what.

00:11:01.535 --> 00:11:02.828
Maybe the killer is in there.

00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:04.497
We don't know.

00:11:04.497 --> 00:11:07.083
But we needed to
establish this and provide

00:11:07.083 --> 00:11:08.542
an atmosphere for the movie.

00:11:08.542 --> 00:11:12.088
And ultimately, what
we decided to do

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:14.256
was what I'm going to
show you right now.

00:11:17.176 --> 00:11:26.644
[SCREAMS]

00:11:26.644 --> 00:11:28.938
Here we are, going over
the top of the screen.

00:11:28.938 --> 00:11:33.275
So you're seeing the image
from the back of the screen.

00:11:33.275 --> 00:11:35.736
And then we establish
the drive-in.

00:11:35.736 --> 00:11:38.739
And we start honing in on
Corey and Jami, who are our two

00:11:38.739 --> 00:11:40.783
principles, our two leads.

00:11:43.744 --> 00:11:46.080
You're not enjoying
this, are you?

00:11:47.498 --> 00:11:50.960
Oh, I just don't like these
kinds of films so much.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:52.837
Why didn't you say so?

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:57.883
We can go.

00:11:57.883 --> 00:11:59.218
Really?

00:11:59.218 --> 00:12:00.469
Do you mean it?

00:12:00.469 --> 00:12:03.723
[SCREAMING]

00:12:05.057 --> 00:12:07.309
OK, so they get into their car.

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:12.106
Pulls us back, and
then we establish,

00:12:12.106 --> 00:12:15.818
here's our two teenage boys.

00:12:15.818 --> 00:12:19.071
Man, last time I saw this
movie was when I was like, 12.

00:12:19.071 --> 00:12:23.284
You see that this is actually
a makeshift drive-in.

00:12:23.284 --> 00:12:24.660
God bless you--

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.830
We see Charles Pierce,
Jr. sitting at this bar.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.291
Creepily, we have no
idea who he is yet.

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:36.088
We start to hear the
reverend's voice.

00:12:36.088 --> 00:12:37.548
Take a look at this, now.

00:12:37.548 --> 00:12:38.966
This is a godless field.

00:12:38.966 --> 00:12:41.469
We see the church
ladies putting flyers

00:12:41.469 --> 00:12:43.846
through that people are ignoring
because they're too busy

00:12:43.846 --> 00:12:44.597
making out.

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:45.973
Come to church on Sunday.

00:12:45.973 --> 00:12:47.057
I'll tell you all about it.

00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:52.396
We see the priest, and
we see the other church

00:12:52.396 --> 00:12:53.439
ladies go back.

00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:56.150
This is KYGL, Oldies
on the Border.

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:57.735
It's Halloween
night, so if you're

00:12:57.735 --> 00:13:00.488
sitting at home waiting for the
trick or treaters to come by,

00:13:00.488 --> 00:13:02.490
we'll keep you company
with our usual bang--

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.493
Real people died,
you know, young man?

00:13:05.493 --> 00:13:08.287
Real people died.

00:13:08.287 --> 00:13:08.829
That's right.

00:13:08.829 --> 00:13:10.247
It's a godless field.

00:13:10.247 --> 00:13:11.707
I'll see you on Sunday.

00:13:11.707 --> 00:13:12.666
You're welcome.

00:13:15.961 --> 00:13:16.670
That's good.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:17.171
You leave.

00:13:17.171 --> 00:13:18.380
This is a godless field.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.632
It's good that you're leaving.

00:13:37.775 --> 00:13:38.567
All right.

00:13:38.567 --> 00:13:41.320
Simple, right?

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.491
That was actually the simplest
way to convey the information,

00:13:45.491 --> 00:13:48.160
and it gave us a
sense of the town.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.329
It gave us a sense of the
young people in the town.

00:13:50.329 --> 00:13:53.207
It introduced,
sketchily, the people

00:13:53.207 --> 00:13:55.417
who would be involved
in this movie.

00:13:55.417 --> 00:13:58.921
And it also gave us
a sense of fluidity,

00:13:58.921 --> 00:14:02.341
because In this movie,
we wanted the camera

00:14:02.341 --> 00:14:05.553
to be able to infiltrate
people's lives.

00:14:05.553 --> 00:14:09.181
This gets to my
point of simplicity

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:11.642
and why it's important.

00:14:11.642 --> 00:14:12.518
KISS.

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:15.020
Normally, that means
"Keep It Short, Stupid."

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:19.066
In this case, it means
"Keep It Simple, Stupid."

00:14:19.066 --> 00:14:22.653
OK, when people are in the quest
of trying to find their style,

00:14:22.653 --> 00:14:25.072
they overcomplicate things.

00:14:25.072 --> 00:14:28.284
They start thinking
in very grandiose,

00:14:28.284 --> 00:14:30.536
difficult to
accomplish ways of how

00:14:30.536 --> 00:14:33.163
to tell the story because
they want to make something

00:14:33.163 --> 00:14:35.207
their visual signature.

00:14:35.207 --> 00:14:38.210
I'm always looking for
what is the simplest

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.881
way to convey the information.

00:14:41.881 --> 00:14:45.009
Well, let me show
you something that's

00:14:45.009 --> 00:14:48.345
really kind of changed my life.

00:14:48.345 --> 00:14:54.810
And this is a scene from a
movie that you've probably

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.396
seen, called The Graduate.

00:14:57.396 --> 00:14:58.439
--way downstairs.

00:14:58.439 --> 00:15:00.232
The Carlsons are here.

00:15:00.232 --> 00:15:00.983
They are?

00:15:00.983 --> 00:15:02.359
Well, come on.

00:15:02.359 --> 00:15:05.571
They came all the
way from Tarzana.

00:15:05.571 --> 00:15:07.406
Come on, let's get cracking.

00:15:07.406 --> 00:15:12.161
It's a wonderful thing to
have so many devoted friends.

00:15:12.161 --> 00:15:14.121
Hey, there's the
award-winning scholar!

00:15:14.121 --> 00:15:15.831
We're all very
proud of you, Ben.

00:15:15.831 --> 00:15:16.832
Thank you, Mrs. Carlson.

00:15:16.832 --> 00:15:18.083
Is it the new car out there?

00:15:18.083 --> 00:15:19.168
That little red Wop job?

00:15:19.168 --> 00:15:20.252
That's Ben's graduation present.

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:21.879
Oh, you won't have much trouble
picking them up in that,

00:15:21.879 --> 00:15:22.379
will you?

00:15:22.379 --> 00:15:22.963
Sir?

00:15:22.963 --> 00:15:23.797
The girls!

00:15:23.797 --> 00:15:25.132
The chicks, the teeny boppers.

00:15:25.132 --> 00:15:27.593
Oh, I think Ben's gotten
beyond the teeny bopper stage,

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:28.344
haven't you, Ben?

00:15:28.344 --> 00:15:30.387
Yes, ma'am.

00:15:30.387 --> 00:15:32.514
Excuse me, I think I'd just
like to check something

00:15:32.514 --> 00:15:34.016
on the car for a minute.

00:15:34.016 --> 00:15:37.561
His father bought
him an Alfa Romeo.

00:15:37.561 --> 00:15:39.563
Hey, here's the track star.

00:15:39.563 --> 00:15:40.814
How are you, track star?

00:15:40.814 --> 00:15:42.149
Just fine, Mr. Loomis.

00:15:42.149 --> 00:15:42.900
You get the point.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.527
OK, so in the script--

00:15:45.527 --> 00:15:46.612
you get the script.

00:15:46.612 --> 00:15:49.448
It reads that there's a
huge party in his honor,

00:15:49.448 --> 00:15:51.867
that everybody's there
drinking and eating

00:15:51.867 --> 00:15:53.035
hors d'eouvres and happy.

00:15:53.035 --> 00:15:55.579
And Benjamin is led
around the room and talks

00:15:55.579 --> 00:15:58.248
to a whole bunch of different
people and stuff like that.

00:15:58.248 --> 00:16:00.709
But what did you notice
about The Graduate?

00:16:00.709 --> 00:16:04.421
Everything in that
scene, first of all,

00:16:04.421 --> 00:16:07.216
is one shot that
was going around.

00:16:07.216 --> 00:16:10.094
The camera was on this
uncomfortably tight close

00:16:10.094 --> 00:16:11.679
up of Dustin Hoffman.

00:16:11.679 --> 00:16:15.015
Everybody else was, like,
half of faces, people

00:16:15.015 --> 00:16:16.934
that were sort of indistinct.

00:16:16.934 --> 00:16:19.812
You didn't really get a
sense of who they were.

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:20.980
What were they doing?

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.107
They were trying to
make the audience feel

00:16:23.107 --> 00:16:25.985
like Dustin Hoffman
felt. They were

00:16:25.985 --> 00:16:30.531
trying to make the audience
feel that disorientation,

00:16:30.531 --> 00:16:33.492
that disinterest, that
disconnectedness with what's

00:16:33.492 --> 00:16:35.411
going on around him.

00:16:35.411 --> 00:16:36.996
They wanted to make
the audience feel

00:16:36.996 --> 00:16:39.331
as uncomfortable as
his character felt.

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:42.376
And if you look at The
Graduate, every single scene

00:16:42.376 --> 00:16:44.753
in The Graduate does that.

00:16:44.753 --> 00:16:48.215
Every single scene is
designed to make you feel

00:16:48.215 --> 00:16:51.010
the way his character feels.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:53.595
That is where your
style comes from.

00:16:53.595 --> 00:16:56.015
I did the exact same
thing-- and this is a thing

00:16:56.015 --> 00:16:57.641
that I do every single time.

00:16:57.641 --> 00:17:00.394
I get the script,
I read it, and then

00:17:00.394 --> 00:17:02.938
I climb into the head
of the characters.

00:17:02.938 --> 00:17:04.773
Who's the main character?

00:17:04.773 --> 00:17:08.068
And then I find some way to
visually depict the world

00:17:08.068 --> 00:17:10.988
as the character feels it.

00:17:10.988 --> 00:17:15.576
That becomes the style of
what it is that you do.

00:17:15.576 --> 00:17:18.579
You're roping in the
audience into making

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:21.415
them be one with the character.

00:17:21.415 --> 00:17:23.584
And the difference with
American Horror Story,

00:17:23.584 --> 00:17:25.961
and the reason why my
cinematography in American

00:17:25.961 --> 00:17:28.255
Horror Story got
so much attention,

00:17:28.255 --> 00:17:30.966
is that every single
character in that show

00:17:30.966 --> 00:17:33.218
was completely fucked up.

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:37.848
Completely dysfunctional,
couldn't deal with reality.

00:17:37.848 --> 00:17:41.060
I was climbing into the
heads of crazy people,

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:43.353
and then visually
depicting the world the way

00:17:43.353 --> 00:17:45.773
crazy people see it.

00:17:45.773 --> 00:17:46.607
OK?

00:17:46.607 --> 00:17:51.570
And that's why this show and
its visual style got attention.

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:56.075
Observation three, never
go with your first idea.

00:17:56.075 --> 00:17:59.661
Never ever, ever go
with your first idea.

00:17:59.661 --> 00:18:01.205
Why?

00:18:01.205 --> 00:18:04.958
It may be a
fantastic first idea.

00:18:04.958 --> 00:18:07.795
I will tell you this-- your
first idea for doing anything

00:18:07.795 --> 00:18:10.047
is probably the most
conventional way

00:18:10.047 --> 00:18:11.590
to do something.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.634
You probably did it
before, if you have

00:18:13.634 --> 00:18:14.927
any experience in the business.

00:18:14.927 --> 00:18:18.097
You did it before, and you
probably did it successfully,

00:18:18.097 --> 00:18:20.390
which is why you
want to do it again,

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:22.893
because everybody
wants to do what

00:18:22.893 --> 00:18:25.938
they know everybody will love.

00:18:25.938 --> 00:18:32.111
If you do that, you end up
endlessly repeating yourself.

00:18:32.111 --> 00:18:35.364
You start doing the same shots
over and over and over again

00:18:35.364 --> 00:18:37.491
because you know
people will like it.

00:18:37.491 --> 00:18:40.160
And then you don't grow
as a creative artist.

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:42.287
You become stagnated.

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:45.082
And then ultimately, you become
that horrible four letter

00:18:45.082 --> 00:18:45.749
word--

00:18:45.749 --> 00:18:47.126
"hack."

00:18:47.126 --> 00:18:50.254
You've become somebody who
just regurgitates and recycles

00:18:50.254 --> 00:18:52.089
the same thing over
and over again.

00:18:52.089 --> 00:18:55.134
And if you want to make
your mark in this industry

00:18:55.134 --> 00:18:56.802
and you want to
survive, you have

00:18:56.802 --> 00:19:00.139
to be somebody who is open
to reinventing yourself

00:19:00.139 --> 00:19:01.181
constantly.

00:19:01.181 --> 00:19:05.144
You say, well, my struggle
is to not compromise.

00:19:05.144 --> 00:19:05.811
No.

00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:07.521
Your struggle is
really to embrace

00:19:07.521 --> 00:19:10.858
compromise because of this.

00:19:10.858 --> 00:19:14.653
There's three types of shots
when you're figuring out

00:19:14.653 --> 00:19:18.240
your coverage, how you're going
to shoot a scene as a director

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.033
or cinematographer.

00:19:20.033 --> 00:19:22.744
And they're the shots that
are your personal artistic

00:19:22.744 --> 00:19:25.289
flourish-- maybe these are
the things that you initially

00:19:25.289 --> 00:19:28.083
thought of that you're
really excited about,

00:19:28.083 --> 00:19:31.378
there are the shots that make
the basic storytelling more

00:19:31.378 --> 00:19:34.590
exciting, and then
there are shots

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:36.425
that simply tell the story.

00:19:36.425 --> 00:19:39.803
The type of shots that you
cannot get rid of ever are

00:19:39.803 --> 00:19:42.347
the shots that tell the story.

00:19:42.347 --> 00:19:44.558
If you don't get the
shots that tell the story

00:19:44.558 --> 00:19:46.268
and make the story
understandable

00:19:46.268 --> 00:19:49.396
and make the audience understand
why the character's feeling

00:19:49.396 --> 00:19:52.608
the way they are, then you
have failed in your job

00:19:52.608 --> 00:19:56.904
as a cinematographer or
director or anything.

00:19:56.904 --> 00:19:59.907
You need to at least
get those shots.

00:19:59.907 --> 00:20:05.204
I cannot tell you how many
times I've worked with directors

00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:10.709
as a cinematographer and they
were going to walk away from

00:20:10.709 --> 00:20:15.672
a scene without getting a shot
that actually tells the story

00:20:15.672 --> 00:20:19.218
and makes that story
understandable.

00:20:19.218 --> 00:20:22.137
Let me show you a
sample of a scene that

00:20:22.137 --> 00:20:25.974
was a complete compromise.

00:20:25.974 --> 00:20:29.436
You sound exactly like
my daughter Scarlett.

00:20:29.436 --> 00:20:33.357
She's around your age, but she's
the oldest little girl I know.

00:20:33.357 --> 00:20:35.150
I think deep down,
she blames herself

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:36.652
for everything
that's wrong with me.

00:20:41.323 --> 00:20:42.741
But it's me.

00:20:43.784 --> 00:20:46.578
Not her.

00:20:46.578 --> 00:20:49.289
And it's him.

00:20:49.289 --> 00:20:52.000
Not you.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:52.751
I like you.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:57.089
The simplest way, OK?

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:59.132
I had no time to do
what I wanted to,

00:20:59.132 --> 00:21:02.261
which was I had this shot
that wrapped around them,

00:21:02.261 --> 00:21:05.806
that rack focus between
him and her as the camera's

00:21:05.806 --> 00:21:08.433
going around, and it
became this matrix,

00:21:08.433 --> 00:21:10.602
this labyrinth of
the two of them

00:21:10.602 --> 00:21:12.271
kind of intertwined
with each other.

00:21:12.271 --> 00:21:13.939
Absolutely no time to do that.

00:21:13.939 --> 00:21:15.399
All right, throw that out.

00:21:15.399 --> 00:21:17.109
What is the second idea?

00:21:17.109 --> 00:21:18.568
What do we do now?

00:21:18.568 --> 00:21:23.156
And in that moment, I told the
operators, OK, this closeup,

00:21:23.156 --> 00:21:25.200
just off frame.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.119
Just give me half of
her face, you know?

00:21:27.119 --> 00:21:29.121
And when we turn
around on Wes, we'll

00:21:29.121 --> 00:21:30.747
just shoot the other
half of the face

00:21:30.747 --> 00:21:34.042
and then I'll do cross
dissolves between them.

00:21:34.042 --> 00:21:37.921
And Ryan Murphy, the creator
of the show, when he saw it--

00:21:37.921 --> 00:21:40.048
originally, the scene
had much more dialogue.

00:21:40.048 --> 00:21:42.426
She had more dialogue about
the abuse she suffered

00:21:42.426 --> 00:21:46.013
from her father, but they
became uncomfortable with having

00:21:46.013 --> 00:21:47.973
her say those words
after we shot it.

00:21:47.973 --> 00:21:49.891
So they removed a bunch
of the dialogue, which

00:21:49.891 --> 00:21:52.853
is why there was a dissolve
where he wasn't saying anything

00:21:52.853 --> 00:21:54.438
and she wasn't saying anything.

00:21:54.438 --> 00:21:56.648
But they wanted
to keep that layer

00:21:56.648 --> 00:21:58.984
of dissolves, which they did.

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:00.902
That became the compromise.

00:22:00.902 --> 00:22:04.614
That became the method by which
I demonstrated to the audience

00:22:04.614 --> 00:22:08.618
that these two people were
getting in sync about how

00:22:08.618 --> 00:22:10.287
they felt about the same thing.

00:22:10.287 --> 00:22:11.371
And it was really simple.

00:22:11.371 --> 00:22:13.415
Just off-frame this
way, off-frame this way,

00:22:13.415 --> 00:22:16.501
and I'll just put dissolves
between the two of them.

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:17.878
So that's the second idea.

00:22:17.878 --> 00:22:19.671
If I'd gone with
the first idea, it

00:22:19.671 --> 00:22:21.923
wouldn't have been
nearly as interesting.

00:22:21.923 --> 00:22:25.969
Observation four--
that's a quote from me.

00:22:25.969 --> 00:22:30.932
"I have created an entire career
of making enormous compromise

00:22:30.932 --> 00:22:34.644
look like an intentional style."

00:22:34.644 --> 00:22:36.188
Absolutely right.

00:22:36.188 --> 00:22:39.191
They'll probably put
that on my tombstone.

00:22:39.191 --> 00:22:42.611
Everything that I did in
my entire career, certainly

00:22:42.611 --> 00:22:45.072
everything I did on
American Horror Story,

00:22:45.072 --> 00:22:46.698
was never my first idea.

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:48.241
It was a compromise.

00:22:48.241 --> 00:22:52.037
It was a compromise because of
lack of money, lack of time,

00:22:52.037 --> 00:22:57.501
lack of equipment, losing
actors at a certain time.

00:22:57.501 --> 00:22:59.503
Everything was a compromise.

00:22:59.503 --> 00:23:05.759
When you compromise, you have
to compromise in bold ways.

00:23:05.759 --> 00:23:11.848
I always say, there's not one
way to light or shoot a scene.

00:23:11.848 --> 00:23:14.017
There's hundreds and
hundreds of ways,

00:23:14.017 --> 00:23:16.186
but people get locked
in to doing it,

00:23:16.186 --> 00:23:17.604
oh, this is such a great idea.

00:23:17.604 --> 00:23:18.855
I need to do it like this.

00:23:18.855 --> 00:23:22.818
And then they short-circuit
their brain from functioning

00:23:22.818 --> 00:23:26.154
by their depression in not
being able to get that,

00:23:26.154 --> 00:23:28.740
and so they try to save it and
they whittle it down and say,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.368
well, OK, what if I
just do this, what if--

00:23:31.368 --> 00:23:31.993
don't do that.

00:23:31.993 --> 00:23:33.036
Throw it away.

00:23:33.036 --> 00:23:33.870
Throw it away.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:34.830
You can't do it.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:36.540
You can't accomplish it.

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.000
You don't have the time
or resources to do it.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.044
Find a different idea.

00:23:41.044 --> 00:23:44.047
So let me go into
inspiration a little bit.

00:23:44.047 --> 00:23:45.590
Where does the
inspiration come from?

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:47.592
Well, it comes from
everywhere, honestly.

00:23:47.592 --> 00:23:49.636
It comes from your
life, it comes

00:23:49.636 --> 00:23:52.681
from works of art, from
photographs, from other movies,

00:23:52.681 --> 00:23:54.224
from paintings.

00:23:54.224 --> 00:23:56.226
But you have to be open to it.

00:23:56.226 --> 00:23:58.770
And people say, well, you
know, I feel uninspired.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.189
And here's a little
exercise for you.

00:24:01.189 --> 00:24:05.318
Tomorrow, you probably get up
off the same side of the bed

00:24:05.318 --> 00:24:07.821
every single day,
I would imagine.

00:24:07.821 --> 00:24:11.283
Tomorrow, get up on a
different side of the bed

00:24:11.283 --> 00:24:14.161
or climb to the end of
the bed and get out of bed

00:24:14.161 --> 00:24:16.371
if you can't get
out from the side.

00:24:16.371 --> 00:24:20.041
Just get out of bed
on a different side.

00:24:20.041 --> 00:24:24.129
All of a sudden, you will feel
like your world has changed.

00:24:24.129 --> 00:24:26.965
Just trying to shake up
your world a little bit

00:24:26.965 --> 00:24:29.342
and expose yourself
to different things.

00:24:29.342 --> 00:24:34.806
And let me show you something
in the vein of change

00:24:34.806 --> 00:24:44.608
for, or using other
inspirations, for what you do.

00:24:44.608 --> 00:24:46.067
This is an artist--

00:24:46.067 --> 00:24:49.779
I'm going to show you a couple
of photos of an artist called

00:24:49.779 --> 00:24:51.531
Gregory Crewdson.

00:24:51.531 --> 00:24:55.827
When we were prepping to
shoot The Town That Dreaded

00:24:55.827 --> 00:24:58.997
Sundown, that clip I showed
you in the drive-in theater,

00:24:58.997 --> 00:25:02.167
Gregory Crewdson's work
became a cornerstone

00:25:02.167 --> 00:25:04.961
of the visual
approach to the show.

00:25:04.961 --> 00:25:08.423
Because you see this interior,
and it has an unreality.

00:25:08.423 --> 00:25:12.886
It has almost an artificiality,
almost a Hollywood kind of feel

00:25:12.886 --> 00:25:15.555
about it, even though
it's very noir-ish.

00:25:15.555 --> 00:25:22.395
And his work, like this,
became part of the signature,

00:25:22.395 --> 00:25:25.148
I think, of the feel of
the show because something

00:25:25.148 --> 00:25:28.860
about his work appealed to
Alfonso Gomez-Rejon, who

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:32.656
was the director, and myself,
who's the cinematographer.

00:25:32.656 --> 00:25:34.991
And so that feeling
was something

00:25:34.991 --> 00:25:39.412
that we wanted to carry
through in the movie.

00:25:39.412 --> 00:25:42.457
And I want to show
you a scene that

00:25:42.457 --> 00:25:47.420
was a direct inspiration from,
actually, that last photograph

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.047
that I showed you.

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:51.299
Because normally,
the inspirations

00:25:51.299 --> 00:25:55.470
don't end up being translated
so directly into the movie.

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:57.180
But this was one
instance where it

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:00.725
did because that
inspiration was so perfect.

00:26:10.151 --> 00:26:12.112
Tomorrow for sure?

00:26:13.655 --> 00:26:18.201
So that photograph became the
perfect image of the feeling

00:26:18.201 --> 00:26:20.912
that I want to get between
these two characters

00:26:20.912 --> 00:26:22.872
at this point in the movie.

00:26:22.872 --> 00:26:24.541
But it took design.

00:26:24.541 --> 00:26:27.377
You know, we put
him in a red jacket

00:26:27.377 --> 00:26:29.713
specifically because
I was going to use

00:26:29.713 --> 00:26:33.967
only that light as the only
source for the entire scene.

00:26:33.967 --> 00:26:36.845
And I didn't want
her face to go blue.

00:26:36.845 --> 00:26:38.763
So we put him in a red
jacket so the light

00:26:38.763 --> 00:26:43.810
that bounced off of him onto her
would be a more natural color.

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:46.479
And then we put
her in a blue top

00:26:46.479 --> 00:26:49.941
because I wanted to keep up the
mystery of him as to whether

00:26:49.941 --> 00:26:52.611
or not he was the killer.

00:26:52.611 --> 00:26:55.905
One thing, if I can impress
upon you, as cinematographers,

00:26:55.905 --> 00:27:00.327
those of you who are
cinematographers, one thing--

00:27:00.327 --> 00:27:03.830
every now and then, I
will work with somebody,

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:07.417
as I'm directing,
who will say, well,

00:27:07.417 --> 00:27:10.045
we can't shoot that way
because the light has

00:27:10.045 --> 00:27:11.004
to come from this way.

00:27:11.004 --> 00:27:12.505
And I'll just say, why?

00:27:12.505 --> 00:27:15.216
And they'll say, because
that's where the windows are

00:27:15.216 --> 00:27:19.512
and that's where the light
would come from in reality.

00:27:19.512 --> 00:27:23.183
And I really kind of feel like,
when did it become our job

00:27:23.183 --> 00:27:26.394
to recreate reality?

00:27:26.394 --> 00:27:29.314
Isn't our job to
create the reality that

00:27:29.314 --> 00:27:31.566
works for the drama,
that works for how

00:27:31.566 --> 00:27:33.151
we're trying to make
the audience feel

00:27:33.151 --> 00:27:34.819
about the characters?

00:27:34.819 --> 00:27:37.989
You have to have the conviction
that this is the way I

00:27:37.989 --> 00:27:39.157
want to tell the story.

00:27:39.157 --> 00:27:41.242
What is the best way
to tell the story?

00:27:41.242 --> 00:27:45.330
How is the audience going
to emotionally plug-in

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:48.708
to what it is that I'm
trying to get them to do?

00:27:48.708 --> 00:27:52.379
There is no such
thing as bad lighting.

00:27:52.379 --> 00:27:54.923
No such thing as bad lighting.

00:27:54.923 --> 00:27:59.594
I totally, totally,
honestly really stress this.

00:27:59.594 --> 00:28:01.346
Let me ask you this--

00:28:01.346 --> 00:28:04.766
if we're doing a scene,
if I'm the main character

00:28:04.766 --> 00:28:09.270
and this is the way I'm lit,
and my line of dialogue is,

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:13.024
"Sweetheart, I love you,"
you could look at this

00:28:13.024 --> 00:28:15.443
and say, wow, that's
really bad lighting.

00:28:15.443 --> 00:28:18.154
But if my line of
dialogue is "I'm

00:28:18.154 --> 00:28:21.408
going to rip your guts
out and kill you,"

00:28:21.408 --> 00:28:26.955
all of a sudden, not only has
the lighting gotten better,

00:28:26.955 --> 00:28:31.000
but it's making
the audience feel

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.129
the angst of that character, OK?

00:28:34.129 --> 00:28:36.756
So when people say, well,
that's bad lighting,

00:28:36.756 --> 00:28:39.384
normally what they're
reacting to is lighting

00:28:39.384 --> 00:28:42.345
that feels somehow
inappropriate for what

00:28:42.345 --> 00:28:44.347
it is they're watching.

00:28:44.347 --> 00:28:48.810
OK, you've violated the world
because you haven't connected

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:52.188
the visual style, the visual
end of what you're trying to do,

00:28:52.188 --> 00:28:55.650
with the emotional end of what
the character is experiencing.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:57.068
There's a disconnect.

00:28:57.068 --> 00:28:59.195
But if you're connected
to the character,

00:28:59.195 --> 00:29:03.533
you can get away
with murder, OK?

00:29:03.533 --> 00:29:06.453
And that's where the
style comes from.

00:29:06.453 --> 00:29:10.165
That's when it becomes
fun to really play

00:29:10.165 --> 00:29:11.291
with those kinds of things.

00:29:11.291 --> 00:29:16.254
And let me show you a
clip from something.

00:29:16.254 --> 00:29:19.549
And I'm going to keep my
mic open because I want

00:29:19.549 --> 00:29:22.886
to talk over this as it goes.

00:29:22.886 --> 00:29:25.638
This is a scene from
American Horror Story,

00:29:25.638 --> 00:29:30.226
and it's an episode where
a third of the episode

00:29:30.226 --> 00:29:36.608
was going to be 16-millimeter
documentary footage.

00:29:36.608 --> 00:29:37.692
You need an angle.

00:29:37.692 --> 00:29:40.320
A hook.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:42.572
And boy, did I have a doozy.

00:29:49.496 --> 00:29:51.539
I heard from very
reliable sources--

00:29:51.539 --> 00:29:55.794
The concept of this,
all this footage,

00:29:55.794 --> 00:29:59.839
was that Sarah Paulson's
character, Lana Winters,

00:29:59.839 --> 00:30:01.716
was going back to
the asylum where

00:30:01.716 --> 00:30:04.803
she was an inmate,
with a documentary crew

00:30:04.803 --> 00:30:07.180
and doing an expose
of the conditions

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:09.516
that they lived there.

00:30:09.516 --> 00:30:13.478
And what Ryan Murphy told
me before we shot this is he

00:30:13.478 --> 00:30:17.232
wanted it to look like
the Geraldo Rivera

00:30:17.232 --> 00:30:23.029
documentary on mental
asylums from the 1970s.

00:30:23.029 --> 00:30:26.032
Give me a thumbs up
if you can hear me.

00:30:26.032 --> 00:30:27.158
OK, great.

00:30:27.158 --> 00:30:31.412
So I started researching on
the internet the Geraldo Rivera

00:30:31.412 --> 00:30:35.291
documentary, and I could
not find a good copy of it

00:30:35.291 --> 00:30:36.251
anywhere.

00:30:36.251 --> 00:30:39.295
Everything that I found was
multiple generations away,

00:30:39.295 --> 00:30:41.339
looked really pukey and awful.

00:30:41.339 --> 00:30:45.051
I could not find a
clean copy anywhere.

00:30:45.051 --> 00:30:47.053
And what I realized
is, wait a minute,

00:30:47.053 --> 00:30:51.599
If I can't find a copy of this,
then probably what Ryan saw

00:30:51.599 --> 00:30:57.188
was a putrid, disgusting looking
copy of this documentary.

00:30:57.188 --> 00:31:00.441
But what I did was I
took 16-millimeter film,

00:31:00.441 --> 00:31:07.240
I underexposed it 3 stops, and I
force processed that negative 3

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:11.744
stops, and then I took all of
that 16-millimeter footage,

00:31:11.744 --> 00:31:15.999
and I had it transferred it to
3/4-inch pneumatic videotape,

00:31:15.999 --> 00:31:19.586
which was an antiquated system
that nobody used anymore,

00:31:19.586 --> 00:31:21.963
those industrial tapes.

00:31:21.963 --> 00:31:26.092
And then I had them transferred
back from 3/4-inch to high

00:31:26.092 --> 00:31:29.053
definition files.

00:31:29.053 --> 00:31:31.931
Your job as
cinematographers, directors,

00:31:31.931 --> 00:31:34.851
is to tap into
that emotional core

00:31:34.851 --> 00:31:37.186
and find the image that
makes the audience feel

00:31:37.186 --> 00:31:41.232
that way, without regard of
what people will tell you,

00:31:41.232 --> 00:31:44.152
this is not proper lighting.

00:31:44.152 --> 00:31:48.781
Observation six, all
leading concepts consist

00:31:48.781 --> 00:31:50.992
of four elements.

00:31:50.992 --> 00:31:53.828
I know the process
of lighting sometimes

00:31:53.828 --> 00:31:56.164
is very difficult for
people to understand.

00:31:56.164 --> 00:31:58.875
And this is very valuable
for those of you, especially

00:31:58.875 --> 00:32:03.296
who are not cinematographers,
because what

00:32:03.296 --> 00:32:05.840
I'm going to explain
to you, probably a lot

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:08.259
of cinematographers
haven't thought of

00:32:08.259 --> 00:32:11.512
or have complicated
to the point where it

00:32:11.512 --> 00:32:13.848
becomes a more complex theory.

00:32:13.848 --> 00:32:15.516
But this is what it is.

00:32:15.516 --> 00:32:18.519
There are only four elements
to lighting, really.

00:32:18.519 --> 00:32:23.608
It's the angle of the light,
the color of the light,

00:32:23.608 --> 00:32:28.237
the intensity of the light,
and the acuity of the light.

00:32:28.237 --> 00:32:30.573
Is it hard or soft?

00:32:30.573 --> 00:32:34.160
Those are the four things
that are manipulated in order

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:41.292
to create the mood,
the effects, just

00:32:41.292 --> 00:32:43.753
the general feel of any scene.

00:32:43.753 --> 00:32:48.091
It's not rocket science,
where what separates

00:32:48.091 --> 00:32:52.512
the artist, who's successful
from the person who is not,

00:32:52.512 --> 00:32:55.598
is the inspiration
and the ability

00:32:55.598 --> 00:32:58.935
to transfer that
inspiration into terms

00:32:58.935 --> 00:33:00.853
that the rest of the
crew can jump on,

00:33:00.853 --> 00:33:03.690
that your gaff, or your key
grip, and everybody else

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:06.943
can plug into; the wardrobe
designer, the costume,

00:33:06.943 --> 00:33:09.404
the production designer.

00:33:09.404 --> 00:33:12.073
They have to be able to
key into the visual concept

00:33:12.073 --> 00:33:13.241
that you are presenting.

00:33:13.241 --> 00:33:15.451
Let me show you something.

00:33:15.451 --> 00:33:18.788
What is not in vogue
very much lately,

00:33:18.788 --> 00:33:22.917
and it's really disappointing
to me, is using hard light.

00:33:22.917 --> 00:33:26.295
Let me show you just a
quick couple of photos.

00:33:26.295 --> 00:33:30.341
So this is lit with soft light.

00:33:30.341 --> 00:33:34.637
This is an actress
lit with soft light.

00:33:34.637 --> 00:33:37.640
And she looks pleasant
enough, but there

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:43.271
are little circles under
her eyes, and you know,

00:33:43.271 --> 00:33:46.315
it's not maybe as
vibrant as I would like.

00:33:46.315 --> 00:33:49.986
This is the same
face with hard light.

00:33:49.986 --> 00:33:53.281
You see her eye color has
gotten more pronounced.

00:33:53.281 --> 00:33:54.115
Why is that?

00:33:54.115 --> 00:33:57.660
Because her pupils
are shrinking,

00:33:57.660 --> 00:34:03.666
and it exposes more of the
color of the rest of her eye.

00:34:03.666 --> 00:34:07.253
You see the lips, the
lipstick on her lips,

00:34:07.253 --> 00:34:11.424
is more pure red in this.

00:34:11.424 --> 00:34:16.512
And look at what's happening
with the bags under her eyes,

00:34:16.512 --> 00:34:19.390
is the lightning is
washing them out.

00:34:19.390 --> 00:34:21.559
And there is no
change in her makeup.

00:34:21.559 --> 00:34:25.480
It's just changing from a soft
light to a hard light source.

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:29.025
Hard light was
the glamour choice

00:34:29.025 --> 00:34:33.237
for lighting actresses for
many, many years in Hollywood.

00:34:33.237 --> 00:34:34.697
And it's fallen out of favor.

00:34:34.697 --> 00:34:36.449
Not many people are
using it anymore,

00:34:36.449 --> 00:34:40.161
and I always loved
lighting, especially women,

00:34:40.161 --> 00:34:41.037
with hard light.

00:34:41.037 --> 00:34:44.290
But today, actresses
start to freak out

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:46.501
if they see hard light
because they think it's harsh.

00:34:46.501 --> 00:34:48.086
And you have to
explain to them, well,

00:34:48.086 --> 00:34:52.215
this is the glamour lighting of
the 1920s, '30s, '40s, and '50s

00:34:52.215 --> 00:34:52.882
in Hollywood.

00:34:52.882 --> 00:34:55.218
This is what makes
you look amazing.

00:34:55.218 --> 00:34:59.180
People always ask, why
did Henry Fonda's eyes in

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:02.767
Once Upon a Time in the West
looks so piercingly blue?

00:35:02.767 --> 00:35:04.102
Did he wear contact lenses?

00:35:04.102 --> 00:35:05.228
No.

00:35:05.228 --> 00:35:10.066
They had a big hard light in
front of his face, beaming

00:35:10.066 --> 00:35:13.111
into his face, and it shrunk
down the pupil so much

00:35:13.111 --> 00:35:16.155
that his blue eyes just popped.

00:35:16.155 --> 00:35:19.575
I'm just going to get into a
little bit about the difference

00:35:19.575 --> 00:35:23.246
between film and digital.

00:35:23.246 --> 00:35:26.082
This is film emulsion.

00:35:26.082 --> 00:35:28.209
What you notice about
the film emulsion is

00:35:28.209 --> 00:35:30.711
that the silver halite
crystals, no two

00:35:30.711 --> 00:35:35.508
are exactly the same shape
or size or color or anything.

00:35:35.508 --> 00:35:39.387
OK, when light strikes
silver halite crystals,

00:35:39.387 --> 00:35:42.056
when light strikes
the emulsion of film,

00:35:42.056 --> 00:35:47.645
they rearrange themselves into a
myriad of kind of permutations.

00:35:47.645 --> 00:35:53.943
And there is an unlimited
number of combinations

00:35:53.943 --> 00:35:56.237
that they can go into, OK?

00:35:56.237 --> 00:35:58.364
So the amount of
information that

00:35:58.364 --> 00:36:01.284
can be stored on a piece
of motion picture film

00:36:01.284 --> 00:36:02.952
is unlimited.

00:36:02.952 --> 00:36:04.912
Here's another view of it.

00:36:04.912 --> 00:36:07.456
The digital sensor is finite.

00:36:07.456 --> 00:36:11.836
It doesn't have an infinite
amount of permutations.

00:36:11.836 --> 00:36:14.964
It has, like, this many
pixels in a sensor,

00:36:14.964 --> 00:36:16.799
or however many it is.

00:36:16.799 --> 00:36:19.302
And it has to resolve
what it is that it's

00:36:19.302 --> 00:36:24.182
going to resolve for the image
within that number of pixels.

00:36:27.059 --> 00:36:31.564
So what happens, then,
when you go into shooting?

00:36:31.564 --> 00:36:34.066
Well, what happened initially
with the initial digital

00:36:34.066 --> 00:36:38.946
cameras, this is a painting
by George Seurat called Sunday

00:36:38.946 --> 00:36:39.530
in the Park.

00:36:39.530 --> 00:36:41.449
And the actual
painting is hanging

00:36:41.449 --> 00:36:44.869
in the Art Institute of
Chicago, and it's the size

00:36:44.869 --> 00:36:46.621
of your apartment wall.

00:36:46.621 --> 00:36:48.289
It's big.

00:36:48.289 --> 00:36:49.707
It's really big.

00:36:49.707 --> 00:36:52.668
But when you walk up
close to it, what you find

00:36:52.668 --> 00:36:56.464
is that the brown of the
skin is not just brown.

00:36:56.464 --> 00:36:59.717
It's got blue, it's got
yellow, it's got red in it.

00:36:59.717 --> 00:37:03.054
The face also has
different pigments.

00:37:03.054 --> 00:37:07.016
The dog, the brown
dog, is not just brown.

00:37:07.016 --> 00:37:10.102
And this is what you're looking
at when you stand back from it.

00:37:13.272 --> 00:37:16.651
Especially early digital
cameras could not resolve color.

00:37:16.651 --> 00:37:21.822
It didn't have the color depth
to do that kind of thing.

00:37:21.822 --> 00:37:26.702
So what it did was it emphasized
the colors that it could.

00:37:26.702 --> 00:37:29.538
So you had green grass
that was very, very

00:37:29.538 --> 00:37:33.042
green, that didn't have other
gradations of color in it.

00:37:33.042 --> 00:37:35.044
You had blue water.

00:37:35.044 --> 00:37:38.881
And people said, well, wow, this
digital camera, this high def

00:37:38.881 --> 00:37:40.132
camera is way too sharp.

00:37:40.132 --> 00:37:40.675
Well, no.

00:37:40.675 --> 00:37:43.261
The problem was that the initial
high def cameras were not

00:37:43.261 --> 00:37:43.970
sharp enough.

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:48.641
They could not store
enough data to give us

00:37:48.641 --> 00:37:52.728
a more rounded, accurate,
realistic picture of what

00:37:52.728 --> 00:37:55.564
it is it was seeing, so
it emphasized the things

00:37:55.564 --> 00:37:56.857
that it could see.

00:37:56.857 --> 00:37:59.402
And that created a
perceived sharpness

00:37:59.402 --> 00:38:04.282
that people misinterpreted
as being sharper than film.

00:38:04.282 --> 00:38:06.284
Part of the reason
why I bring that up

00:38:06.284 --> 00:38:12.290
is because you shouldn't get
obsessed with chasing Ks.

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:15.001
Everybody is like,
ah, well, this is 2K.

00:38:15.001 --> 00:38:16.711
Yeah, but I've got 4K.

00:38:16.711 --> 00:38:20.923
Yeah, but I can do this in 8K.

00:38:20.923 --> 00:38:25.845
The Ks don't matter as much
for you during production.

00:38:25.845 --> 00:38:28.222
I mean, they probably
matter a lot in post,

00:38:28.222 --> 00:38:32.435
but you should be choosing
the medium that you shoot on

00:38:32.435 --> 00:38:35.771
based on, aesthetically,
does it give you

00:38:35.771 --> 00:38:39.066
what it is that you
want for that project?

00:38:39.066 --> 00:38:41.736
Does it convey what it
is that you want to say?

00:38:41.736 --> 00:38:43.696
Because our palette,
as filmmakers,

00:38:43.696 --> 00:38:47.199
should be wide open to
use whatever they want to,

00:38:47.199 --> 00:38:52.580
and not just limited to you
have to use this 8K camera.

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:54.749
Observation number seven.

00:38:54.749 --> 00:38:57.668
Don't sit at video village.

00:38:57.668 --> 00:39:00.046
Just don't.

00:39:00.046 --> 00:39:02.298
And I don't care if
you're the director

00:39:02.298 --> 00:39:05.593
or the cinematographer,
just don't do that.

00:39:05.593 --> 00:39:09.180
It's really a pet peeve
for me that people--

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.640
we've grown up now,
we're growing up now

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:13.601
in this culture
of people who sit

00:39:13.601 --> 00:39:15.895
at video village with a
cappuccino in their hands

00:39:15.895 --> 00:39:18.898
and headphones on their
heads in a comfy chair,

00:39:18.898 --> 00:39:20.399
and then, if they're
directors, they

00:39:20.399 --> 00:39:24.779
scream direction over a wall
from another room at actors.

00:39:24.779 --> 00:39:27.865
Directing is not
a passive sport.

00:39:27.865 --> 00:39:30.368
It's a contact sport, OK?

00:39:30.368 --> 00:39:35.831
You need to be on your feet,
those of you who are directors.

00:39:35.831 --> 00:39:37.500
I don't believe the
director should ever

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:38.876
be sitting down and directing.

00:39:38.876 --> 00:39:41.087
I don't believe that a
cinematographer should be

00:39:41.087 --> 00:39:43.464
stuck in a black tent all day.

00:39:43.464 --> 00:39:47.093
When you live inside
the tent, when

00:39:47.093 --> 00:39:49.762
you live inside the
black tent with the DIT,

00:39:49.762 --> 00:39:53.307
as a cinematographer,
you lose the connectivity

00:39:53.307 --> 00:39:54.558
with the actors.

00:39:54.558 --> 00:39:56.852
There's always been such
a great relationship

00:39:56.852 --> 00:39:59.146
between cinematographers
and actors.

00:39:59.146 --> 00:40:02.650
And that's starting to erode
because the cinematographer

00:40:02.650 --> 00:40:04.527
spends all of their
time in the tent.

00:40:04.527 --> 00:40:05.986
I think that's a mistake.

00:40:05.986 --> 00:40:09.782
100% of my concentration
is on the performers.

00:40:09.782 --> 00:40:12.535
100%, OK?

00:40:12.535 --> 00:40:14.912
And I stand there six
feet away from them,

00:40:14.912 --> 00:40:16.497
and I watch them
while they perform.

00:40:16.497 --> 00:40:18.624
I can't tell you how
many television actors

00:40:18.624 --> 00:40:23.921
have told me, oh my god, you're
always there by the camera.

00:40:23.921 --> 00:40:26.632
Even if we can't see you because
you don't stand in our eyeline,

00:40:26.632 --> 00:40:28.259
but we know you're
always there, and I

00:40:28.259 --> 00:40:30.886
feel like my performance
has consequences.

00:40:30.886 --> 00:40:33.264
I feel like my
performance is important

00:40:33.264 --> 00:40:35.850
because I'm not being
yelled at from another room

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:39.353
or being told direction
or adjustments

00:40:39.353 --> 00:40:44.150
from an AD, a second
AD, on a walkie-talkie.

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:45.985
That's important, OK?

00:40:45.985 --> 00:40:48.237
That's how you get the
performance that you want.

00:40:48.237 --> 00:40:51.282
And that's how you make
the set go very fast.

00:40:51.282 --> 00:40:54.326
And then when I
direct the actors,

00:40:54.326 --> 00:40:56.787
after I get a take that I love--

00:40:56.787 --> 00:40:58.706
and it could be take number one.

00:40:58.706 --> 00:41:02.418
With Gary Oldman, 40% of the
time, it was take number one.

00:41:02.418 --> 00:41:06.589
After I get a performance
that I love, and I call cut,

00:41:06.589 --> 00:41:11.260
I immediately ask the
crew, is everybody good?

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:13.512
And the operators,
the assistants,

00:41:13.512 --> 00:41:15.181
and the dolly
pushers especially,

00:41:15.181 --> 00:41:16.974
they know what I mean.

00:41:16.974 --> 00:41:21.103
If anything went wrong in their
part of what we were doing,

00:41:21.103 --> 00:41:23.981
all they need to do
is tell me right away,

00:41:23.981 --> 00:41:25.399
"I need another one."

00:41:25.399 --> 00:41:29.195
It's just a fast way to work,
and it's a great connectivity.

00:41:29.195 --> 00:41:31.864
I also like to cut.

00:41:31.864 --> 00:41:34.867
I love the word "cut."

00:41:34.867 --> 00:41:39.371
Today, they're like, let it
roll, keep the camera rolling.

00:41:39.371 --> 00:41:41.916
I'm just going to go in
there and adjust something.

00:41:41.916 --> 00:41:43.042
Keep rolling.

00:41:43.042 --> 00:41:46.545
So excuse me, why?

00:41:46.545 --> 00:41:47.588
Why?

00:41:47.588 --> 00:41:51.509
In post, if they have to
sift through 40 minutes

00:41:51.509 --> 00:41:56.013
of a running camera to find
the 20 seconds that was good,

00:41:56.013 --> 00:42:01.560
they are spending their time
researching and not editing.

00:42:01.560 --> 00:42:03.687
You need to give
them less footage.

00:42:03.687 --> 00:42:07.483
You need to give them
more good footage.

00:42:07.483 --> 00:42:11.403
So "cut" is a valuable word
that seems to be disappearing

00:42:11.403 --> 00:42:12.863
from the lexicon.

00:42:12.863 --> 00:42:13.948
OK.

00:42:13.948 --> 00:42:15.449
What are unsafe hours?

00:42:15.449 --> 00:42:17.368
It's the hours that you
work that contribute

00:42:17.368 --> 00:42:19.537
to fatigue of the crew.

00:42:19.537 --> 00:42:22.873
What happens when you
work a significant number

00:42:22.873 --> 00:42:28.420
of consecutive long-hour days,
14, 15, 16 hours every day?

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:30.798
Well, three things happen.

00:42:30.798 --> 00:42:34.468
The creativity suffers,
the crew gets exhausted,

00:42:34.468 --> 00:42:36.804
safety goes out the
window, and it's

00:42:36.804 --> 00:42:39.265
expensive to pay for
all the overtime.

00:42:39.265 --> 00:42:40.975
In a lot of cases,
the scripts are

00:42:40.975 --> 00:42:42.810
written too large
to be accomplished

00:42:42.810 --> 00:42:45.271
in the amount of time that
the studio or the network

00:42:45.271 --> 00:42:46.438
wants them done in.

00:42:46.438 --> 00:42:49.233
And the directors are
saying, oh, OK, well,

00:42:49.233 --> 00:42:50.609
what's pull the plug time?

00:42:50.609 --> 00:42:54.113
We pull the plug in 14 hours,
we pull the plug in 16 hours.

00:42:54.113 --> 00:42:54.780
Oh, great.

00:42:54.780 --> 00:42:56.782
I've got 16 hours to go.

00:42:56.782 --> 00:42:57.616
No.

00:42:57.616 --> 00:42:58.617
No.

00:42:58.617 --> 00:42:59.451
No.

00:42:59.451 --> 00:43:01.829
Wrong way to approach it.

00:43:01.829 --> 00:43:04.957
In 25 episodes that I've
directed of television

00:43:04.957 --> 00:43:08.377
in the last 3 and 1/2 years,
I have not had a single day

00:43:08.377 --> 00:43:10.379
go over 12 hours.

00:43:10.379 --> 00:43:13.424
Most of my days are 10
and 1/2 to 12 hours.

00:43:13.424 --> 00:43:16.594
I won't say that I won't
work over 12 hours,

00:43:16.594 --> 00:43:21.181
because there may be extenuating
circumstances where you

00:43:21.181 --> 00:43:23.017
have to work that 13th hour.

00:43:23.017 --> 00:43:25.227
But all the crews
that I work with

00:43:25.227 --> 00:43:29.023
know that when that
12-hour mark comes,

00:43:29.023 --> 00:43:31.859
I'll probably be in my van
going back to the airport

00:43:31.859 --> 00:43:33.444
or to the hotel.

00:43:36.447 --> 00:43:38.991
You have to portion
out your day.

00:43:38.991 --> 00:43:41.785
And I want to see a show
of hands, how many of you

00:43:41.785 --> 00:43:43.746
love interviewing for a job?

00:43:43.746 --> 00:43:47.374
Honestly, raise your hands.

00:43:47.374 --> 00:43:52.129
Coco, Jose, Carley,
and Gabriel, and Dan.

00:43:52.129 --> 00:43:55.507
OK, and the rest of you, I take
it-- oh, Milan and Caterina.

00:43:55.507 --> 00:43:56.550
Good, good.

00:43:56.550 --> 00:43:59.345
Let me tell you this--

00:43:59.345 --> 00:44:03.057
interviewing for a
job in this industry

00:44:03.057 --> 00:44:09.480
is my favorite thing to do in
the world, truly and honestly.

00:44:09.480 --> 00:44:13.400
If I could get paid to
just interview for jobs

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:15.986
and never have to
do the job, I would

00:44:15.986 --> 00:44:18.489
be the happiest guy on Earth.

00:44:18.489 --> 00:44:21.992
The job interview
is my time to play.

00:44:21.992 --> 00:44:23.661
And the reason why
it's my time to play

00:44:23.661 --> 00:44:28.165
is because it's the only
time that job is perfect.

00:44:28.165 --> 00:44:32.127
It's the only time you're not
worried about budget schedules,

00:44:32.127 --> 00:44:34.088
difficult actors, whatever.

00:44:34.088 --> 00:44:36.674
It's the only time
that job is perfect.

00:44:36.674 --> 00:44:38.842
So I love the job interview.

00:44:38.842 --> 00:44:40.969
What are they trying
to figure out?

00:44:40.969 --> 00:44:42.763
Those of you who don't
like job interviews,

00:44:42.763 --> 00:44:45.849
what are they trying to figure
out when they interview you

00:44:45.849 --> 00:44:47.226
for a job?

00:44:47.226 --> 00:44:51.522
There's only two things,
and two things only.

00:44:51.522 --> 00:44:57.444
They want to know if they like
you, and if they trust you.

00:44:57.444 --> 00:44:58.821
That's it.

00:44:58.821 --> 00:45:01.281
That's what they're trying
to learn in a job interview.

00:45:01.281 --> 00:45:03.575
And when people go
into a job interview

00:45:03.575 --> 00:45:06.120
and they're freaked
out, you know,

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:09.456
they come in, and
when the people say--

00:45:09.456 --> 00:45:12.167
the first question is
always, always simple.

00:45:12.167 --> 00:45:19.174
And it's usually, so, Alejandra,
what have you been up to?

00:45:19.174 --> 00:45:23.429
And a lot of people will just
start reciting their resume.

00:45:23.429 --> 00:45:26.932
Well, I shot this
movie, and I did this,

00:45:26.932 --> 00:45:30.894
and I worked as an electrician
on this, and stuff--

00:45:30.894 --> 00:45:31.603
no.

00:45:31.603 --> 00:45:37.276
This is your opportunity to tell
them something about you that

00:45:37.276 --> 00:45:40.195
speaks to your
passion as a person,

00:45:40.195 --> 00:45:42.656
as a creative person
in this industry.

00:45:42.656 --> 00:45:44.533
I went to one job
interview where they said,

00:45:44.533 --> 00:45:46.243
so Michael, what
have you been up to?

00:45:46.243 --> 00:45:49.455
And I said, I just built a
railroad train around my house.

00:45:49.455 --> 00:45:50.956
And they're like, what?

00:45:50.956 --> 00:45:54.334
I said, I just jackhammered all
this cement around my house,

00:45:54.334 --> 00:45:57.629
I laid in steel rail,
I repoured the cement,

00:45:57.629 --> 00:45:59.548
and I have railroad
trains, electric trains,

00:45:59.548 --> 00:46:02.718
now that we can ride
around the house.

00:46:02.718 --> 00:46:04.928
And they said, do you
have pictures of this?

00:46:04.928 --> 00:46:06.388
And I said, yes, I do.

00:46:06.388 --> 00:46:08.891
And I showed them
pictures of my trains.

00:46:08.891 --> 00:46:11.727
And they said, what
motivated you to do that?

00:46:11.727 --> 00:46:14.855
And I talked about how when
I was a kid, we had no money

00:46:14.855 --> 00:46:17.649
and I always imagined
I had a train,

00:46:17.649 --> 00:46:21.111
but I'd draw train tracks with
chalk around our apartment

00:46:21.111 --> 00:46:22.780
building in Chicago.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:25.866
And I told my wife, I
want to build a train.

00:46:25.866 --> 00:46:28.160
And she's like, don't
you think the kids

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:29.578
are going to be
too old for trains

00:46:29.578 --> 00:46:30.621
by the time you finish this?

00:46:30.621 --> 00:46:32.414
I said, well, it's not
really for the kids.

00:46:32.414 --> 00:46:33.499
It's for me.

00:46:33.499 --> 00:46:34.625
And she says, OK.

00:46:34.625 --> 00:46:36.460
Let's build your railroad then.

00:46:36.460 --> 00:46:39.880
We talked for a half
hour about my train,

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:42.341
and we never talked
about the job.

00:46:42.341 --> 00:46:44.259
And they hired me.

00:46:44.259 --> 00:46:47.471
They are trying to find
out if they like you

00:46:47.471 --> 00:46:49.473
and if they trust you, OK?

00:46:49.473 --> 00:46:51.975
And the other thing people say
when they go into interviews,

00:46:51.975 --> 00:46:54.228
they say, well, I'm afraid
I don't have the experience

00:46:54.228 --> 00:46:55.646
that they're looking for.

00:46:55.646 --> 00:46:56.980
Let me tell you this--

00:46:56.980 --> 00:47:00.192
people will forgive
a lack of experience.

00:47:00.192 --> 00:47:02.986
They will not forgive
a lack of confidence.

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:05.030
And I don't mean, like,
bullshit confidence

00:47:05.030 --> 00:47:06.365
like bravado and stuff.

00:47:06.365 --> 00:47:12.329
I'm talking about if
Madison goes in for a job

00:47:12.329 --> 00:47:17.918
and Destiny goes in for the
same job, and Madison is like--

00:47:17.918 --> 00:47:20.462
and they say, well, you need
to do this and this and this.

00:47:20.462 --> 00:47:23.882
And she says,
well, I don't know.

00:47:23.882 --> 00:47:25.259
I've never tried that.

00:47:25.259 --> 00:47:26.593
I mean, I'll try.

00:47:26.593 --> 00:47:28.804
I mean, I hope I
don't mess it up.

00:47:28.804 --> 00:47:32.224
And Destiny goes in and
says, you know what?

00:47:32.224 --> 00:47:35.769
Honestly, I've never done
that before, but I learn fast

00:47:35.769 --> 00:47:38.272
and I work hard and I
will make it happen.

00:47:38.272 --> 00:47:40.816
Which person do
you want to hire?

00:47:40.816 --> 00:47:44.111
So you have to go into that job
interview with your conviction.

00:47:44.111 --> 00:47:45.654
If you're the
cinematographer, you've

00:47:45.654 --> 00:47:46.947
gotten the script probably.

00:47:46.947 --> 00:47:48.115
You've gone through it.

00:47:48.115 --> 00:47:49.533
You've made your notations.

00:47:49.533 --> 00:47:51.618
Maybe you've brought
in visual samples,

00:47:51.618 --> 00:47:54.538
which is great, on your
computer that you can show them.

00:47:54.538 --> 00:47:56.874
This is how I see this.

00:47:56.874 --> 00:47:59.626
I did one job interview--
and people are always

00:47:59.626 --> 00:48:02.379
afraid, well, what if my
vision doesn't match theirs?

00:48:02.379 --> 00:48:05.674
I tell you, I guarantee
you, nine times out of 10,

00:48:05.674 --> 00:48:07.718
they don't have a vision yet.

00:48:07.718 --> 00:48:11.013
They are looking at you to
come in and give them vision.

00:48:11.013 --> 00:48:12.180
So you do that.

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:15.517
You do that unapologetically,
you do that completely,

00:48:15.517 --> 00:48:17.019
concisely.

00:48:17.019 --> 00:48:18.979
There was one job
interview, I went to

00:48:18.979 --> 00:48:21.815
with a director who directs
$100 million movies.

00:48:21.815 --> 00:48:23.609
It was for a pilot.

00:48:23.609 --> 00:48:29.573
And I said, wow, I see
this program like 1970s

00:48:29.573 --> 00:48:31.909
kung fu movies
stylistically, but bad,

00:48:31.909 --> 00:48:34.036
low budget 1970s kung fu movies.

00:48:34.036 --> 00:48:37.039
I see the camera, like, zooming
in erratically but having

00:48:37.039 --> 00:48:40.876
to re-frame on the fly because
they didn't get the composition

00:48:40.876 --> 00:48:42.502
right and they didn't
have enough money

00:48:42.502 --> 00:48:43.837
to do a second take and stuff.

00:48:43.837 --> 00:48:46.214
I think it has that
kind of nervous energy.

00:48:46.214 --> 00:48:48.383
And he listened to me talk
for, like, five minutes,

00:48:48.383 --> 00:48:52.763
and he said wow, I don't
see it like that at all.

00:48:52.763 --> 00:48:54.848
But you really
thought about this,

00:48:54.848 --> 00:48:56.600
so we should work
together and figure out

00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:58.143
what the style actually is.

00:48:58.143 --> 00:48:59.186
And we did.

00:48:59.186 --> 00:49:00.812
I worked on that show.

00:49:00.812 --> 00:49:03.440
You have to come in
with a vision, committed

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:05.817
to your vision, OK?

00:49:05.817 --> 00:49:09.279
And when people are
in the job interview,

00:49:09.279 --> 00:49:12.449
and especially when you
start getting up in level

00:49:12.449 --> 00:49:14.451
and working on
larger productions,

00:49:14.451 --> 00:49:17.496
there's not just one person
that you're interviewing with.

00:49:17.496 --> 00:49:20.165
It's like a room
of seven people.

00:49:20.165 --> 00:49:22.376
People start to get freaked out.

00:49:22.376 --> 00:49:25.337
And they sit there, and they
answer the first question,

00:49:25.337 --> 00:49:28.757
and then they read the room.

00:49:28.757 --> 00:49:32.177
They look around,
like, who's nodding,

00:49:32.177 --> 00:49:37.057
who's shaking their head,
who's scratching on their pad?

00:49:37.057 --> 00:49:40.060
And then because you're trying
to calibrate what it is you're

00:49:40.060 --> 00:49:42.062
going to say next,
because you want

00:49:42.062 --> 00:49:48.193
to say what they want to hear,
big mistake for two reasons.

00:49:48.193 --> 00:49:50.904
Number one, they
know you're lying.

00:49:50.904 --> 00:49:52.364
They know that
you don't actually

00:49:52.364 --> 00:49:54.700
believe the words that are
coming out of your mouth.

00:49:54.700 --> 00:49:57.828
They know that you're trying
to say things that whatever are

00:49:57.828 --> 00:49:59.913
you think they want to hear.

00:49:59.913 --> 00:50:01.415
They know that.

00:50:01.415 --> 00:50:02.582
You're not actors.

00:50:02.582 --> 00:50:04.209
You can't put that over.

00:50:04.209 --> 00:50:06.086
So you have to give that up.

00:50:06.086 --> 00:50:08.213
Don't read the room.

00:50:08.213 --> 00:50:10.257
And the second
thing that happens--

00:50:10.257 --> 00:50:12.843
and this is the most damaging--

00:50:12.843 --> 00:50:16.346
is that they become
convinced that you do not

00:50:16.346 --> 00:50:19.850
have an original creative
idea of your own,

00:50:19.850 --> 00:50:22.060
and then you're
dead in the room,

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:24.521
and the interview
is officially over.

00:50:24.521 --> 00:50:28.275
It may go on for another
minute, out of courtesy,

00:50:28.275 --> 00:50:32.029
but they're done with you
once they've decided that.

00:50:32.029 --> 00:50:33.530
So don't do that.

00:50:33.530 --> 00:50:35.115
Don't read the room.

00:50:35.115 --> 00:50:36.700
Go in there with
your point of view,

00:50:36.700 --> 00:50:39.745
go in there with your
vision, express it to them.

00:50:39.745 --> 00:50:43.123
It's such an important
part of what you're

00:50:43.123 --> 00:50:47.127
going to do when you
leave this program, when

00:50:47.127 --> 00:50:49.755
you go leave school or
whatever and you go off

00:50:49.755 --> 00:50:51.298
to do your things.

00:50:51.298 --> 00:50:55.635
You need to get comfortable
with the job interview, OK?

00:50:55.635 --> 00:50:57.304
And more and more
important, agents.

00:50:57.304 --> 00:50:59.890
Everybody talks about agents,
or I need the perfect reel,

00:50:59.890 --> 00:51:02.392
or whatever like that.

00:51:02.392 --> 00:51:05.437
Tell you, that face to face
encounter is more important.

00:51:05.437 --> 00:51:08.690
You need to cultivate all
of these relationships

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:09.816
with people.

00:51:09.816 --> 00:51:11.902
And if you're the
kind of person who

00:51:11.902 --> 00:51:13.612
doesn't like to call
people because you

00:51:13.612 --> 00:51:18.033
think you're bugging them,
especially you need to do it.

00:51:18.033 --> 00:51:20.368
So you need to get
contact information,

00:51:20.368 --> 00:51:23.705
and people are very
afraid to do that.

00:51:23.705 --> 00:51:26.124
You have to get over
that fear because it's

00:51:26.124 --> 00:51:27.209
your responsibility.

00:51:27.209 --> 00:51:30.504
If you don't do that,
you remain invisible.

00:51:30.504 --> 00:51:33.924
And invisible is not what you
want to be in this business,

00:51:33.924 --> 00:51:35.217
OK?

00:51:35.217 --> 00:51:37.969
Let me just cover a couple
of other quick things,

00:51:37.969 --> 00:51:41.306
and then we'll get
into to some questions.

00:51:41.306 --> 00:51:44.601
99% of the people
in this business who

00:51:44.601 --> 00:51:48.772
want to get into this business
will not produce anything,

00:51:48.772 --> 00:51:52.609
will not do anything, will not
step out of their comfort zone.

00:51:52.609 --> 00:51:54.236
And I'll tell you this--

00:51:54.236 --> 00:51:58.698
it's not fear of failure
that stops people.

00:51:58.698 --> 00:52:00.784
It is fear of success.

00:52:00.784 --> 00:52:03.829
When you set yourself a goal,
and let's say you get there,

00:52:03.829 --> 00:52:04.621
is that it?

00:52:04.621 --> 00:52:05.705
Is at the end?

00:52:05.705 --> 00:52:06.373
No.

00:52:06.373 --> 00:52:08.542
It's the beginning,
because that opens up

00:52:08.542 --> 00:52:10.377
to other possibilities.

00:52:10.377 --> 00:52:12.796
So I have to choose between
this one and this one,

00:52:12.796 --> 00:52:14.256
and you're like, uh, shit.

00:52:14.256 --> 00:52:16.341
OK, I'll choose this one,
so you follow that path.

00:52:16.341 --> 00:52:18.218
And let's say that that's
the right decision.

00:52:18.218 --> 00:52:18.802
Is that it?

00:52:18.802 --> 00:52:19.469
No.

00:52:19.469 --> 00:52:21.346
Because that leads
to this, and you

00:52:21.346 --> 00:52:24.516
have to make another
decision, which way do I go?

00:52:24.516 --> 00:52:30.605
Success is an ongoing,
complete cycle

00:52:30.605 --> 00:52:33.400
of constantly stepping
into the things

00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:36.528
that you've never done before,
the experiences that you've

00:52:36.528 --> 00:52:41.074
never had, the things that
you're not sure if you can do,

00:52:41.074 --> 00:52:43.994
and going in and doing it.

00:52:43.994 --> 00:52:44.786
OK?

00:52:44.786 --> 00:52:47.247
And what did Yoda say?

00:52:47.247 --> 00:52:50.458
Yoda said "Do, or do not.

00:52:50.458 --> 00:52:53.670
There is no try."

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:55.672
Don't try to do something.

00:52:55.672 --> 00:52:57.007
Do it.

00:52:57.007 --> 00:52:58.258
Commit to it.

00:52:58.258 --> 00:52:59.593
Do it.

00:52:59.593 --> 00:53:03.638
The last point I would
like to make, get a life.

00:53:03.638 --> 00:53:06.057
You're creative people
and whatever you do,

00:53:06.057 --> 00:53:08.727
whatever your field of what
you want to pursue in the film

00:53:08.727 --> 00:53:12.689
business is, you can only
do that if you have a life

00:53:12.689 --> 00:53:14.524
outside of the film business.

00:53:14.524 --> 00:53:17.444
Because if you cut yourself
off from life experiences,

00:53:17.444 --> 00:53:19.571
then what is going
to happen is you

00:53:19.571 --> 00:53:21.948
will have no stories to tell.

00:53:21.948 --> 00:53:25.869
You will have no experiences
to draw from for your art.

00:53:25.869 --> 00:53:28.955
You need to have a life
outside of this business.

00:53:28.955 --> 00:53:31.917
So does anybody have any
questions they'd like to ask?

00:53:31.917 --> 00:53:35.712
Leeann Leonard, one of our
cinematography track students.

00:53:35.712 --> 00:53:36.213
Hey, Leeann.

00:53:36.213 --> 00:53:36.713
Hi.

00:53:36.713 --> 00:53:41.718
I wanted to know, can you
manipulate the principal light

00:53:41.718 --> 00:53:44.012
with mood as much as possible?

00:53:44.012 --> 00:53:46.932
So there's an enormous
amount of manipulation

00:53:46.932 --> 00:53:49.184
you can do with a
principal light source.

00:53:49.184 --> 00:53:53.480
And like I said, I very rarely
use more than three lights.

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:55.857
Early on in my career, I
was using the whole truck

00:53:55.857 --> 00:53:58.652
full of lights, and I found
that when they were shutting off

00:53:58.652 --> 00:54:00.654
the lights after the
scene was finished,

00:54:00.654 --> 00:54:02.572
the scene looked better.

00:54:02.572 --> 00:54:05.450
So I said, OK, I'm going
to start with nothing.

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:07.619
If I walk in and
it looks great, I'm

00:54:07.619 --> 00:54:09.329
just going to start
shooting, which

00:54:09.329 --> 00:54:12.874
drives the ADs crazy, because
oh man, we figured you're going

00:54:12.874 --> 00:54:14.084
to take a half hour to light.

00:54:14.084 --> 00:54:15.835
No, I'm done.

00:54:15.835 --> 00:54:18.213
Just waiting on you.

00:54:18.213 --> 00:54:18.713
Hello, Sir.

00:54:18.713 --> 00:54:19.214
Thank you.

00:54:19.214 --> 00:54:21.675
Thank you for your
time, as well.

00:54:21.675 --> 00:54:25.637
My question is which
is a better approach,

00:54:25.637 --> 00:54:28.598
is it better to have
the camera movement

00:54:28.598 --> 00:54:32.352
or dictate the blocking, or
just block the actors first

00:54:32.352 --> 00:54:36.106
and then choose the camera
movements depending on them?

00:54:36.106 --> 00:54:39.359
Sometimes, I have a
movement in my head

00:54:39.359 --> 00:54:41.236
because I feel like
that movement expresses

00:54:41.236 --> 00:54:42.487
something about the character.

00:54:42.487 --> 00:54:44.364
But when I do the
blocking, sometimes

00:54:44.364 --> 00:54:47.951
where the actor is going
or what we worked out

00:54:47.951 --> 00:54:50.996
is not accommodating
to that movement.

00:54:50.996 --> 00:54:52.998
So I just come up
with something else.

00:54:52.998 --> 00:54:57.794
I love the actors who
challenge me as a director

00:54:57.794 --> 00:55:00.839
and as a cinematographer,
but I love the ones

00:55:00.839 --> 00:55:03.758
who question everything.

00:55:03.758 --> 00:55:06.845
Sometimes they're the ones
that the ADs or the producers

00:55:06.845 --> 00:55:08.680
don't like because they
question everything.

00:55:08.680 --> 00:55:11.850
I love those people because
all those questions are never

00:55:11.850 --> 00:55:12.976
coming from ego.

00:55:12.976 --> 00:55:15.812
They're coming from their
passion and their commitment

00:55:15.812 --> 00:55:18.023
to their character and
what they want to do.

00:55:18.023 --> 00:55:20.400
And that helps me drive
forward, ultimately,

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:21.860
what that scene becomes.

00:55:21.860 --> 00:55:23.862
Thank you so much, Michael.

00:55:23.862 --> 00:55:24.821
Thank you.

00:55:24.821 --> 00:55:27.240
I think we can all
unmute ourselves and tell

00:55:27.240 --> 00:55:28.575
Michael how amazing he is.

00:55:28.575 --> 00:55:30.410
That was so informative.

00:55:30.410 --> 00:55:34.080
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:34.080 --> 00:55:36.249
Thank you so much.

00:55:36.249 --> 00:55:37.000
That was awesome.

00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:37.500
Thank you so much, Michael.

00:55:37.500 --> 00:55:38.251
You're the best, man.

00:55:38.251 --> 00:55:38.835
Thank you.

00:55:38.835 --> 00:55:40.045
Thank you so much.

00:55:40.045 --> 00:55:40.795
Farewell.

00:55:40.795 --> 00:55:42.672
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:42.672 --> 00:55:43.340
--Michael.

00:55:43.340 --> 00:55:44.132
Thank you.

00:55:44.132 --> 00:55:45.091
Awesome.

00:55:45.091 --> 00:55:47.844
I'm expecting amazing
things out of you.

00:55:47.844 --> 00:55:50.722
I mean, you're in the
Academy Gold program.

00:55:50.722 --> 00:55:55.352
I mean, this is not some
slouchy, second second-hand fly

00:55:55.352 --> 00:55:55.852
by.

00:55:55.852 --> 00:55:57.062
This is the Academy.

00:55:57.062 --> 00:56:00.357
You need to step up and
do amazing things, OK?

00:56:00.357 --> 00:56:00.857
Thank you.

00:56:00.857 --> 00:56:01.524
Thank you.

00:56:01.524 --> 00:56:02.025
Thank you.

00:56:02.025 --> 00:56:03.234
Thank you so much.

